uncovered ones belong to L \\\\cup R.\\n\\nA rectangle that lies entirely inside L must be left‑aligned: if the rectangle occupies rows I \\\\subseteq \\\\{1,…,n\\\\} and columns [c_1,c_2], then for every i \\\\in I we must have c_2 \\\\le a_i, otherwise the rectangle would contain a square to the right of the uncovered square in that row, which is forbidden. Consequently the rightmost column of such a rectangle equals a_i for some row i that it covers.\\n\\nSimilarly, a rectangle that lies entirely inside R must be right‑aligned: if it occupies rows I and columns [c_1,c_2], then for every i \\\\in I we must have c_1 \\\\ge \\\\sigma(i)+1, i.e., c_1 \\\\ge n-b_i+1. Hence the leftmost column of the rectangle equals n-b_i+1 for some row i that it covers.\\n\\nWe now prove a lower bound.\\n\\n**Lemma 1.** At least n-1 rectangles are required to cover L.\\n*Proof.* For each k \\\\in \\\\{1,2,…,n-1\\\\} there is a unique row i_k with a_{i_k}=k, because the a_i are a permutation of {0,1,…,n-1}. Consider the square s_k = (i_k, k), which is the rightmost square of row i_k that belongs to L. Any rectangle covering s_k must have its rightmost column equal to k; if its rightmost column were smaller than k the square s_k would not be covered, and if it were larger than k the rectangle would contain the square (i_k, k+1), which does not belong to L. Moreover, a rectangle whose rightmost column is k cannot cover any square s_{k'} with k'\\\\neq k, because for k' > k the square s_{k'} lies in column k' > k, which lies outside the rectangle, and for k' < k the square s_{k'} lies in a row whose a_i = k' < k, so the rectangle would have to extend to column k' which is to the left of its rightmost column, but that is allowed; however, the rectangle would then also contain the square (i_{k'}, k), which does not belong to L (because a_{i_{k'}} = k' < k, so column k is to the right of the uncovered square in that row). Hence a rectangle with rightmost column k cannot contain any other square s_{k'} with k'\\\\neq k. Consequently each k forces a distinct rectangle, and we need at least n-1 rectangles to cover all squares of L. ∎\\n\\n**Lemma 2.** At least n-1 rectangles are required to cover R.\\n*Proof.* The argument is symmetric. For each k \\\\in \\\\{1,2,…,n-1\\\\} there is a unique row j_k with b_{j_k}=k. The leftmost square of row j_k that belongs to R is t_k = (j_k, n-k+1). Any rectangle covering t_k must have its leftmost column equal to n-k+1; otherwise the rectangle would either miss t_k or would contain a square to the left of the uncovered square in that row, which is forbidden. As in Lemma\\\\,1, a rectangle whose leftmost column is n-k+1 cannot contain any other square t_{k'} with k'\\\\neq k. Hence we need at least n-1 rectangles for R. ∎\\n\\nAdding the two lemmas we obtain the universal lower bound\\n\\\\[ \\\\#\\\\text{tiles} \\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. \\\\]\\n\\n**Construction attaining the bound.** Choose the identity permutation \\\\sigma(i)=i for all i. Then a_i = i-1 and b_i = n-i. For each i=2,\\\\dots,n place a horizontal rectangle of size 1\\\\times(i-1) covering row i and columns 1,2,\\\\dots,i-1. For each i=1,\\\\dots,n-1 place a horizontal rectangle of size 1\\\\times(n-i) covering row i and columns i+1,i+2,\\\\dots,n. All these rectangles are pairwise disjoint, they cover every square of the board except the n diagonal squares (i,i), and their total number is (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\nThus the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals 2n-2. For n=2025 we obtain\\n\\\\[ 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048. \\\\]\\nHence the answer is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4049\n}"
