\in\\\\{2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ let $i_k$ be the unique row with $p(i_k)=k$ (such a row exists because $p$ is a permutation).  The cell\\n\\\\[\\n(i_k,\\\\,k-1)\\\\in L\\n\\\\]\\nis the rightmost cell of row $i_k$ that lies left of its uncovered square.\\n\\n**Claim.**  The $n-1$ cells $(i_k,k-1)$, $k=2,\\\\dots,n$, belong to pairwise distinct tiles.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Suppose a single tile $T$ contains both $(i_k,k-1)$ and $(i_{\\\\ell},\\\\ell-1)$ with $k<\\\\ell$.  Since $T$ is a rectangle, its rightmost column is at most $\\\\min\\\\{k-1,\\\\ell-1\\\\}=k-1$.  Consequently $T$ cannot contain the cell $(i_{\\\\ell},\\\\ell-1)$ because its column $\\\\ell-1$ is strictly larger than $k-1$.  Hence no tile can contain two of the listed cells, proving the claim.  ∎\\n\\nThus at least $n-1$ tiles are required to cover $L$.  An analogous argument applied to the *right* part\\n\\\\[\\nR=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le n,\\\\;p(i)<j\\\\le n\\\\}\\n\\\\]\\nuses the cells $(i_k,k+1)$ for $k=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ and shows that at least $n-1$ tiles are needed for $R$ as well.  Since $L$ and $R$ are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board needs at least\\n\\\\[\\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\n\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly $2n-2$ tiles\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nWe now exhibit a construction that attains the lower bound for **every** permutation $p$.\\n\\n##### 2.1  Covering the left side $L$\\nDefine the left heights\\n\\\\[\\nh_i:=p(i)-1\\\\qquad(1\\\\le i\\\\le n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe sequence $(h_i)$ forms a skyline: in row $i$ the cells of $L$ occupy the first $h_i$ columns.\\n\\nWe decompose this skyline into rectangles by the classic \\\"stack\\\" algorithm (also known as the algorithm that computes the area of a histogram).  The procedure works as follows.\\n\\n```\\nInitialize an empty stack S.\\nFor i=1 to n:\\n    let h = h_i.\\n    start = i.\\n    while S is not empty and top(S).height > h:\\n        (h_top, row_top) = pop(S).\\n        // a rectangle of width h_top - h, height i - row_top\\n        output rectangle  [1, h_top] × [row_top, i-1].\\n        start = row_top.\\n    push (h, start) onto S.\\nAfter the loop, while S is not empty:\\n    (h_top, row_top) = pop(S).\\n    output rectangle [1, h_top] × [row_top, n].\\n```\\n\\nEach time a height $h_{\\\\text{top}}$ is popped, we create a rectangle whose rightmost column is $h_{\\\\text{top}}$ and whose rows are a maximal contiguous block in which $h_{\\\\text{top}}$ is the smallest left height.  Because the heights $h_i$ are all distinct (they are $0,1,\\\\dots,n-1$ in some order), every positive height $1,2,\\\\dots,n-1$ is popped exactly once.  Consequently the algorithm produces **exactly $n-1$ rectangles**, all of which lie completely inside $L$ (they never reach column $h_i+1$, the column of the uncovered square in their rows).  The rectangles are pairwise disjoint because their row intervals are disjoint, and together they cover every cell of $L$.\\n\\n##### 2.2  Covering the right side $R$\\nThe right side is symmetric.  Define the right heights\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\tilde h_i:=n-p(i)\\\\qquad(1\\\\le i\\\\le n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe cells of $R$ in row $i$ occupy the last $\\\\tilde h_i$ columns.  Applying the same stack algorithm to the reversed sequence $(\\\\tilde h_i)$ (or, equivalently, to the original board after a $180^{\\\\circ}$ rotation) yields **exactly $n-1$ disjoint rectangles** that fill $R$.\\n\\n##### 2.3  The complete tiling\\nThe $n-1$ rectangles covering $L$ and the $n-1$ rectangles covering $R$ are disjoint (they lie on opposite sides of the permutation squares) and together cover every unit square that is not in $U$.  Hence we obtain a tiling of the whole $n\\\\times n$ board with exactly\\n\\\\[\\n2(n-1)=2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048\\n\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n#### 3.  Conclusion\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nWe have proved a lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles and exhibited a construction that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles for any admissible placement of uncovered squares.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n**Answer:** \\\\boxed{4048}\\n\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4040\n}"
