und of $2n-2$ tiles\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFor each column $k$ with $2\\\\le k\\\\le n$ let $i_k$ be the unique row such that $p(i_k)=k$ (the row of the uncovered square in column $k$).  The cell\\n\\\\[\\nL_k\\\\;:=\\\\;(i_k,\\\\,k-1)\\n\\\\]\\nlies immediately to the left of that uncovered square; we call it a *left cell*.  Similarly, for each column $\\\\ell$ with $1\\\\le\\\\ell\\\\le n-1$ let $j_{\\\\ell}$ be the row with $p(j_{\\\\ell})=\\\\ell$, and define the *right cell*\\n\\\\[\\nR_{\\\\ell}\\\\;:=\\\\;(j_{\\\\ell},\\\\,\\\\ell+1).\\n\\\\]\\nBoth $L_k$ and $R_{\\\\ell}$ are covered squares.\\n\\n**Claim 1.**  No rectangle can contain two distinct left cells.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle $T$ contains $L_k$ and $L_{k'}$ with $k<k'$.  Its rightmost column is at most $k-1$, but $L_{k'}$ lies in column $k'-1>k-1$, a contradiction.  Hence the claim.  \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n\\n**Claim 2.**  No rectangle can contain two distinct right cells.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Symmetric to Claim 1.  \\\\(\\\\square\\\\)\\n\\nThus each left cell must belong to a distinct tile, and each right cell must belong to a distinct tile.  There are $n-1$ left cells and $n-1$ right cells, so at least $2n-2$ tiles are needed **unless** a single tile can contain one left cell *and* one right cell.\\n\\nWe now exhibit a set of $2n-2$ covered squares for which such a mixed tile is impossible.\\n\\nFor $i=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ define\\n\\\\[\\nA_i\\\\;:=\\\\;(i,\\\\,p(i+1)),\\\\qquad B_i\\\\;:=\\\\;(i+1,\\\\,p(i)).\\n\\\\]\\nBecause $p$ is a permutation, $p(i)\\\\neq p(i+1)$.  Consequently, in each pair $\\\\{A_i,B_i\\\\}$ at most one of the two squares can be uncovered: if $p(i+1)=i$ then $A_i=(i,i)$ is uncovered and $B_i=(i+1,i+1)$ is covered, and vice‑versa.  From each pair we keep the square that is covered; denote the resulting $2n-2$ squares by $S$.\\n\\n**Claim 3.**  Any axis‑parallel rectangle that contains two distinct squares of $S$ must also