q k. Consequently each k forces a distinct rectangle, and we need at least n-1 rectangles to cover all squares of L. ∎\\n\\n**Lemma 2.** At least n-1 rectangles are required to cover R.\\n*Proof.* The argument is symmetric. For each k \\\\in \\\\{1,2,…,n-1\\\\} there is a unique row j_k with b_{j_k}=k. The leftmost square of row j_k that belongs to R is t_k = (j_k, n-k+1). Any rectangle covering t_k must have its leftmost column equal to n-k+1; otherwise the rectangle would either miss t_k or would contain a square to the left of the uncovered square in that row, which is forbidden. As in Lemma\\\\,1, a rectangle whose leftmost column is n-k+1 cannot contain any other square t_{k'} with k'\\\\neq k. Hence we need at least n-1 rectangles for R. ∎\\n\\nAdding the two lemmas we obtain the universal lower bound\\n\\\\[ \\\\#\\\\text{tiles} \\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. \\\\]\\n\\n**Construction attaining the bound.** Choose the identity permutation \\\\sigma(i)=i for all i. Then a_i = i-1 and b_i = n-i. For each i=2,\\\\dots,n place a horizontal rectangle of size 1\\\\times(i-1) covering row i and columns 1,2,\\\\dots,i-1. For each i=1,\\\\dots,n-1 place a horizontal rectangle of size 1\\\\times(n-i) covering row i and columns i+1,i+2,\\\\dots,n. All these rectangles are pairwise disjoint, they cover every square of the board except the n diagonal squares (i,i), and their total number is (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\nThus the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals 2n-2. For n=2025 we obtain\\n\\\\[ 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048. \\\\]\\nHence the answer is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4049\n}"
