\\n\\nIf a rectangle R_R has leftmost column n-k+1 and also covers another square t_{k'} with k' \\\\neq k, a similar contradiction arises: for k' < k the square t_{k'} lies in a column > n-k+1, outside the rectangle; for k' > k the column n-k+1 lies to the left of the uncovered square in row j_{k'}, so the rectangle would contain a square not belonging to R. Hence each k forces a distinct rectangle, and at least n-1 rectangles are needed for R. \\\\qed\\n\\nAdding Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we obtain the universal lower bound\\n\\\\[ \\\\#\\\\text{tiles} \\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. \\\\]\\n\\n**Construction attaining the bound.** Choose the identity permutation \\\\sigma(i)=i for all i. Then a_i = i-1 and b_i = n-i. For each i = 2,3,\\\\dots,n place a horizontal rectangle of size 1\\\\times(i-1) covering row i and columns 1,2,\\\\dots,i-1. For each i = 1,2,\\\\dots,n-1 place a horizontal rectangle of size 1\\\\times(n-i) covering row i and columns i+1,i+2,\\\\dots,n. All these rectangles are pairwise disjoint, because each rectangle occupies a distinct row and its column interval does not intersect the column interval of any other rectangle in the same row. Moreover, no rectangle contains any diagonal square (i,i), which are exactly the uncovered squares.\\n\\n**Explicit verification of coverage.** Fix a row i.\\n\\n- If i = 1 then a_1 = 0, so L contains no squares in row 1. The right rectangle placed on row 1 covers precisely the squares (1,j) with j = 2,3,\\\\dots,n, which are exactly the squares of R in that row (since b_1 = n-1).\\n\\n- If 2 \\\\le i \\\\le n-1 then the left rectangle on row i covers the squares (i,j) with 1 \\\\le j \\\\le i-1; these are precisely the squares of L in that row because a_i = i-1. The right rectangle on row i covers the squares (i,j) with i+1 \\\\le j \\\\le n; these are precisely the squares of R in that row because b_i = n-i.\\n\\n- If i = n then b_n = 0, so R contains no squares in row n. The left rectangle on row n covers the squares (n,j) with 1 \\\\le j \\\\le n-1, which are exactly the squares of L in that row (since a_n = n-1).\\n\\nThus every square belonging to L \\\\cup R is covered by exactly one rectangle, and the n diagonal squares (i,i) remain uncovered. The total number of rectangles is (n-1) left rectangles plus (n-1) right rectangles, i.e. 2n-2.\\n\\nSince the lower bound 2n-2 is attained, it is optimal. For n = 2025 we obtain\\n\\\\[ \\\\text{minimum number of tiles} = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048. \\\\]\\n\\nHence the answer is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\"method\":\"final\",\"final_answer\":4049}"
