, \\\\dots, b_n\\\\}.\\n\\nThe squares that must be covered split into two disjoint sets\\n\\\\[ L = \\\\{(i,j) : 1 \\\\le i \\\\le n,\\\\; 1 \\\\le j \\\\le a_i\\\\}, \\\\qquad R = \\\\{(i,j) : 1 \\\\le i \\\\le n,\\\\; \\\\sigma(i)+1 \\\\le j \\\\le n\\\\}. \\\\]\\nAll squares of the board except the uncovered ones belong to L \\\\cup R.\\n\\nConsider any axis‑aligned rectangle T placed on the board. Let its column interval be [c_1,c_2] (with c_1 \\\\le c_2) and let its row set be I \\\\subseteq \\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}. For each i \\\\in I the rectangle occupies the squares (i,j) with c_1 \\\\le j \\\\le c_2.\\n\\nIf for some i \\\\in I we have c_2 > a_i, then column c_2 is to the right of the uncovered square in row i, so the rectangle also contains column \\\\sigma(i)=a_i+1, which is the uncovered square itself—this is forbidden. Similarly, if for some i \\\\in I we have c_1 \\\\le a_i, then column c_1 lies on the left side of the uncovered square in that row, which is allowed only if the rectangle stays entirely on the left side. Consequently a rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares must satisfy either\\n\\\\[ c_2 \\\\le a_i \\\\text{ for every } i \\\\in I, \\\\]\\nin which case the rectangle lies completely in L, or\\n\\\\[ c_1 \\\\ge \\\\sigma(i)+1 \\\\text{ for every } i \\\\in I, \\\\]\\nin which case the rectangle lies completely in R. It cannot have some rows where it lies on the left and other rows where it lies on the right, because then we would have both c_2 \\\\le a_i and c_1 \\\\ge \\\\sigma(j)+1 for some i,j, which would imply c_1 \\\\le c_2 \\\\le a_i < \\\\sigma(j)+1 \\\\le c_2, an impossibility. Hence each rectangle belongs entirely to L or entirely to R.\\n\\n**Lemma 1.** At least n-1 rectangles are required to cover L.\\n\\n*Proof.* For each k \\\\in \\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\} there is a unique row i_k with a_{i_k}=k, because the a_i form a permutation of \\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}. Consider the square s_k = (i_k, k), which is the rightmost square of row i_k that belongs to L. Any rectangle that covers s_k must have its rightmost column equal to k: if its rightmost column were < k, the square s_k would be uncovered; if it were > k, then the rectangle would also contain column k+1 in row i_k, which equals \\\\sigma(i_k) and is the uncovered square—this is forbidden. Therefore the rightmost column of any rectangle covering s_k is exactly k.\\n\\nNow suppose a rectangle R_L has rightmost column k and also covers another square s_{k'} with k' \\\\neq k. If k' > k then s_{k'} lies in column k' > k, which lies outside the column interval of R_L, a contradiction. If k' < k then in row i_{k'} we have a_{i_{k'}} = k' < k, so column k lies to the right of the uncovered square in that row; consequently column k is not part of L for row i_{k'}, and R_L would contain a square that does not belong to L (nor to R), contradicting the fact that a rectangle covering L cannot contain such a square. Hence a rectangle whose rightmost column is k can cover only the square s_k (and possibly squares in rows whose a_i also equal k, but such rows do not exist because the a_i are distinct). Consequently each value k \\\\in \\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\} forces a distinct rectangle. Thus at least n-1 rectangles are needed to cover all squares of L. \\\\qed\\n\\n**Lemma 2.** At least n-1 rectangles are required to cover R.\\n\\n*Proof.* The argument is symmetric. For each k \\\\in \\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\} there is a unique row j_k with b_{j_k}=k. The leftmost square of row j_k that belongs to R is\\n\\\\[ t_k = (j_k, \\\\sigma(j_k)+1) = (j_k, n-k+1). \\\\]\\nAny rectangle covering t_k must have its leftmost column equal to n-k+1: if its leftmost column were > n-k+1, the square t_k would be uncovered; if it were < n-k+1, then the rectangle would also contain column n-k, which lies to the left of the uncovered square in row j_k and therefore is not in R. Hence the leftmost column of any rectangle covering t_k is exactly n-k+1.\\n\\nI