 $V_c$.  Thus the $2n-2$ tiles cover all $n^2-n$ cells that are not on the diagonal, and each row and each column still has exactly one uncovered cell (the diagonal one).  Hence a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles exists.\\n\\n**4. A lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles.**\\nConsider again the diagonal configuration.  Any rectangle $R$ used in a tiling must avoid all diagonal squares.  Let the rows covered by $R$ be the interval $[a,b]$ and the columns covered be $[c,d]$ (both inclusive).  Since $R$ cannot contain a diagonal square, for every $i\\\\in[a,b]$ we must have $i\\\\notin[c,d]$.  Consequently either $b<c$ (all rows lie strictly above all columns) or $a>d$ (all rows lie strictly below all columns).  In the first case we say that $R$ lies *above* the diagonal; in the second case $R$ lies *below* the diagonal.\\n\\n*Above the diagonal.*  Define the set\\n\\\\[\\nA=\\\\{\\\\,(i,i+1)\\\\mid i=1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nEach element of $A$ lies above the diagonal.  Suppose a rectangle $R$ above the diagonal contains two distinct points $(i,i+1)$ and $(k,k+1)$ with $i<k$.  Then $R$\u2019s row interval satisfies $[a,b]\\\\supseteq[i,k]$, so $b\\\\ge k$, while its column interval satisfies $[c,d]\\\\supseteq[i+1,k+1]$, so $c\\\\le i+1$.  Hence $b\\\\ge k\\\\ge i+1\\\\ge c$, contradicting the requirement $b<c$ for a rectangle above the diagonal.  Therefore **no rectangle above the diagonal can contain more than one element of $A$**.  Since $|A|=n-1$, at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed to cover all cells above the diagonal.\\n\\n*Below the diagonal.*  By symmetry consider the set\\n\\\\[\\nB=\\\\{\\\\,(i,i-1)\\\\mid i=2,3,\\\\dots,n\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nEach element of $B$ lies below the diagonal, and the same argument shows that a rectangle below the diagonal can contain at most one element of $B$.  Hence at least $n-1$ rectangles are required to cover the cells below the diagonal.\\n\\nAdding the two lower bounds we obtain that any tiling of the whole board must use at least\\n\\\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n**5. Minimality and the answer for $n=2025$.**\\nThe construction of Section\u202f3 uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles, and Section\u202f4 proves that no tiling can use fewer.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2$.\\nFor $n=2025$ we have\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the required minimum number of tiles is \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\n### End of Solution ###\",\n  \"method\": \"final_answer\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4040\n}"
