rners.*  For each $k\\\\in\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ consider the grid point $(k,k)$.  This point lies on the diagonal and is a corner of the board’s interior boundary: the board contains the cell $(k,k+1)$ to its right (in the upper region) and the cell $(k+1,k)$ below it (in the lower region).  In any tiling, the cell $(k,k+1)$ must be covered by some rectangle $R$ that lies entirely above the diagonal.  As argued in Section 3, any rectangle above the diagonal that contains the cell $(k,k+1)$ must have its bottom row equal to $k$ and its leftmost column equal to $k+1$; otherwise it would either miss the cell or would intersect the diagonal.  Consequently the lower‑left corner of $R$ is the grid point $(k+1,k+1)$.  Hence the inner corner $(k+1,k+1)$ is a corner of the rectangle covering $(k,k+1)$.  An analogous argument shows that the inner corner $(k,k)$ is a corner of the rectangle covering the cell $(k+1,k)$ in the lower region.\\n\\n*At most one inner corner per rectangle.*  Let $R$ be a rectangle lying entirely above the diagonal.  Its row interval is $[r_1,r_2]$ and its column interval $[c_1,c_2]$ with $r_2<c_1$.  The only grid points of the form $(t,t)$ that can belong to the boundary of $R$ are its lower‑left corner $(r_2+1,c_1)$.  Indeed, the other three corners have coordinates $(r_1,c_1)$, $(r_1,c_2+1)$ and $(r_2+1,c_2+1)$; each of these has either row > column or column > row, so it cannot be of the form $(t,t)$.  Hence $R$ can contain **at most one** inner corner $(t,t)$.  The same reasoning applies to a rectangle below the diagonal: its only possible inner corner is its upper‑right corner, so it also contains at most one inner corner.\\n\\n*Counting inner corners.*  There are $n-1$ inner corners $(k,k)$ with $1\\\\le k\\\\le n-1$ that lie on the diagonal but are not the extreme corners $(0,0)$ or $(n,n)$.  As shown above, each inner corner must be a corner of some rectangle (the rectangle covering the adjacent cell in the appropriate region).  Since 