g of the two regions using exactly $2n-2$ rectangles.\\n\\n*Upper region.*  For each $i\\\\in\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ define the horizontal tile\\n\\\\[\\nH_i=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\\\dots,n\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n$H_i$ is a $1\\\\times (n-i)$ rectangle; it lies entirely above the diagonal because its only row is $i$ and its leftmost column is $i+1>i$.\\n\\n*Lower region.*  For each $j\\\\in\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ define the vertical tile\\n\\\\[\\nV_j=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid i=j+1,j+2,\\\\dots,n\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n$V_j$ is a $(n-j)\\\\times1$ rectangle; it lies entirely below the diagonal because its only column is $j$ and its topmost row is $j+1>j$.\\n\\n**Verification of coverage.**\\nTake any cell $(r,c)$ with $r<c$ (i.e. a cell of $U_T$).  Since $c\\\\ge r+1$, the cell belongs to $H_r$, because $H_r$ contains exactly the cells $(r,j)$ with $j\\\\ge r+1$.  Thus every cell of the upper region is covered by exactly one horizontal tile.\\n\\nTake any cell $(r,c)$ with $r>c$ (i.e. a cell of $L_T$).  Since $r\\\\ge c+1$, the cell belongs to $V_c$, because $V_c$ contains exactly the cells $(i,c)$ with $i\\\\ge c+1$.  Thus every cell of the lower region is covered by exactly one vertical tile.\\n\\n**Verification of disjointness.**\\nThe horizontal tiles $H_i$ are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows.  The vertical tiles $V_j$ are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct columns.  Suppose a cell $(i,j)$ belonged to both some $H_{i}$ and some $V_{j}$.  Then $j>i$ (since $H_i$ uses columns $i+1\\\\dots n$) and simultaneously $i>j$ (since $V_j$ uses rows $j+1\\\\dots n$), a contradiction.  Hence no cell is covered by both a horizontal and a vertical tile.  Consequently the $2n-2$ tiles are disjoint and together cover all $n^2-n$ non‑diagonal cells.\\n\\nThus a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles exists.\\n\\n**5. A lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles.**\\nWe now prove that no tiling can use fewer tiles.\\n\\n*Inner corners.*  For each $k\\\\in\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ consider the grid point $(k,k)$.  This point lies on the diagonal and is a corner of the board’s interior boundary: the board contains the cell $(k,k+1)$ to its right (in the upper region) and the cell $(k+1,k)$ below it (in the lower region).  In any tiling, the cell $(k,k+1)$ must be covered by some rectangle $R$ that lies entirely above the diagonal.  Let $R$ have row interval $[r_1,r_2]$ and column interval $[c_1,c_2]$.  Because $R$ contains $(k,k+1)$ we have $r_1\\\\le k\\\\le r_2$ and $c_1\\\\le k+1\\\\le c_2$.  Since $R$ is above the diagonal we have $r_2<c_1$.  From $k\\\\le r_2<c_1\\\\le k+1$ we deduce $r_2=k$ and $c_1=k+1$.  Hence the lower‑left corner of $R$ is the grid point $(k+1,k+1)$.  An analogous argument shows that the rectangle covering the cell $(k+1,k)$ (which lies in the lower region) has its upper‑right corner at the grid point $(k,k)$.  Therefore each inner diagonal point $(k,k)$ (for $1\\\\le k\\\\le n-1$) is a corner of some rectangle.\\n\\n*At most one inner corner per rectangle.*  Let $R$ be a rectangle lying entirely above the diagonal.  Its row interval is $[r_1,r_2]$ and column interval $[c_1,c_2]$ with $r_2<c_1$.  The four corners of $R$ are $(r_1,c_1)$, $(r_1,c_2+1)$, $(r_2+1,c_1)$ and $(r_2+1,c_2+1)$.  Among these, the only one that can have equal coordinates is $(r_2+1,c_1)$, because $r_2+1\\\\le c_1$ and equality holds exactly when $c_1=r_2+1$.  Thus a rectangle above the diagonal can contain **at most one** inner diagonal point, namely its lower‑left corner.  A rectangle below the diagonal is symmetric: its only possible inner diagonal point is its upper‑right corner.  Hence any rectangle can be responsible for at most one inner corner.\\n\\n*Counting inner corners.*  There are $n-1$ inner diagonal points $(k,k)$ with $1\\\\le k\\\\le n-1$.  As shown above, each of them must be a corner of a rectangle, and each rectangle can serve at most one such point.  Consequently at least $n-1$ rectangles are required to cover the upper region, and at least $n-1$ rectangles are required to cover the lower region.  Therefore any tiling of the whole board needs at least\\n\\\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\\\]\\nrectangles.\\n\\n**6. Minimality and the answer for $n=2025$.**\\nThe construction of Section 4 uses exactly $2n-2$ rectangles, and the lower‑bound argument of Section 5 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2$.\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the required minimum number of tiles is \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\",\"method\":\"final_answer\",\"final_answer\":4040}"
