nt (k+1, k+1). An analogous argument shows that the rectangle covering the cell (k+1,k) (which lies in the lower region) has its upper-right corner at the grid point (k,k). Therefore each inner diagonal point (k,k) for 1 <= k <= n-1 is a corner of some rectangle.\\n\\nAt most one inner corner per rectangle.\\nLet R be a rectangle lying entirely above the diagonal. Its row interval is [r1, r2] and column interval [c1, c2] with r2 < c1. The four corners of R are (r1,c1), (r1,c2+1), (r2+1,c1), (r2+1,c2+1). Among these, the only one that can have equal coordinates is (r2+1, c1), because r2+1 <= c1 and equality holds exactly when c1 = r2+1. Thus a rectangle above the diagonal can contain at most one inner diagonal point, namely its lower-left corner. A rectangle below the diagonal is symmetric: its only possible inner diagonal point is its upper-right corner. Hence any rectangle can be responsible for at most one inner corner.\\n\\nCounting inner corners.\\nThere are n-1 inner diagonal points (k,k) with 1 <= k <= n-1. As shown above, each of them must be a corner of a rectangle, and each rectangle can serve at most one such point. Consequently at least n-1 rectangles are required to cover the upper region, and at least n-1 rectangles are required to cover the lower region. Therefore any tiling of the whole board needs at least (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 rectangles.\\n\\n6. Minimality and the answer for n = 2025.\\nThe construction of Section 4 uses exactly 2n-2 rectangles, and the lower-bound argument of Section 5 shows that no tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is 2n-2.\\nFor n = 2025 we obtain\\n2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048.\\nThus the required minimum number of tiles is 4048.\\n\\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\\nBefore finalizing the output, carefully review the Summary and Detailed Solution sections to ensure they are clear, rigorous, and contain no LaTeX, backslashes, or other prohibited symbols. Verify that every claim is justified and that the final answer field matches the derived result 4048.\",\n  \"method\": \"permutation matrix reduction and rectangle tiling with counting argument\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4048\n}"
